"""VO beats for How to Buy a Piper PA-28 (Ep7, N8395H, 1981 Piper PA-28-181 Archer II).
Story cut (Ep3 v2 / Ep4-6 shape): cold open + title -> choose -> ladder -> shortlist -> the one we ran -> report sections as questions -> offer -> keep watching -> verdict.
Plain-language standard (Alex 2026-10-09): every number gets the pattern, a "because" that names a person, and the so-what.
Numbers as words, no ellipses, sentences of about 20 words or fewer."""
BEATS = [
("b01", "title", "This airplane has flown more than twelve thousand hours. Its engine has flown one hundred seventy-four. And it asks about eighty thousand dollars more than other high-hour Archers. This is How to Buy a Piper PA-28. One real airplane for sale right now, one real NextPlane report, and the questions a careful buyer asks."),
("b02", "problem", "Step one. Choose which one. The PA-28 is Piper's four-seat, low-wing family, built since the early sixties. Many pilots learned to fly in one. This week, NextPlane is tracking about three hundred thirty PA-28s for sale in the United States. About two hundred seventy show a price."),
("b03", "ladder", "The first question is which version, because the version sets the price. We use the typical asking price: half ask more, half ask less. The basic Cherokee one forty and one sixty ask about seventy-five thousand dollars. The Cherokee one eighty, about ninety-nine thousand. The Warrior, about one hundred ten thousand. The Arrow, whose wheels fold up in flight, about one hundred twenty-eight thousand. The Archer, about one hundred thirty-nine thousand. The bigger Dakota, about two hundred ten thousand. And late-model Archers, built from twenty eleven on, ask about three hundred sixty to four hundred thirty-five thousand."),
("b04", "shortlist", "Now narrow it down. Say you want an Archer: one hundred eighty horsepower, built from the mid seventies to the mid eighties. Twenty-one of them show a price. Half ask between about one hundred five and one hundred sixty-five thousand dollars. One of the highest asks belongs to one of the highest-hour airplanes. So we ran that one."),
("b05", "pick", "This one. A nineteen eighty-one Archer Two, sold by a private seller in Florida, asking one hundred eighty thousand dollars. The airframe shows twelve thousand three hundred sixty-four hours. That is more than twice the market average. Hours like that often come from years of training or rental flying. But the engine was rebuilt at the factory, and shows one hundred seventy-four hours. And the panel is new Garmin glass, with an autopilot."),
("b06", "header", "Step two. Evaluate the one. Open the NextPlane Aircraft Report. The top section is the summary, with warnings and good news in one place. No accident record. No loan recorded against it in the last year. The same company has owned it for over six years. And one warning: a wing spar inspection. We will come back to that. First, is the price fair?"),
("b07", "value", "This section is the valuation, NextPlane's estimate of what a similar airplane is worth today. The estimate is about one hundred twenty-five thousand dollars. This one asks fifty-five thousand more. But the estimate makes no adjustment for hours, so we checked ourselves. Seven Archers for sale have more than nine thousand hours. Their typical ask is about one hundred thousand dollars. Six Archers with nearly new engines ask a typical one hundred sixty-seven thousand. This one asks more than both groups."),
("b08", "hours", "So what is the extra money buying? This section compares hours with the market. The airframe has about sixty-eight hundred more hours than average. The engine has about a thousand fewer. That matters, because an engine overhaul is a big bill. The report's cost per mile shows it: about one dollar twenty-two a mile, against about one dollar forty-four for a typical PA-28."),
("b09", "engine", "How much is a fresh engine worth? The report sets aside about nineteen dollars an hour for the next engine overhaul. Compared with the high-hour Archers, this engine has almost fifteen hundred more hours of life. At that rate, that is worth roughly twenty-eight thousand dollars. So the engine explains about a third of the eighty thousand gap. The rest is the panel, the autopilot, paint and interior. Decide what those are worth to you before you call."),
("b10", "flight", "Is it flying? This section shows how much the airplane really flies, from NextPlane's flight data. Over two years, we see twenty-six flights and about nineteen hours. In the last twelve months, under ten hours. That fits the engine: about one hundred seventy-four hours in roughly five years. Engines that sit tend to corrode inside. So at the pre-buy, ask for an oil analysis and a camera inspection of the cylinders."),
("b11", "pricehist", "Has the seller moved? This section is the listing history, every price change NextPlane recorded. Listed on October third at two hundred twenty thousand dollars. Six days later, cut by forty thousand. Of twenty-five Archers listed this spring and summer, eight cut their price. Their typical cut was about six percent. Nine were still listed after ninety days. This seller moved faster and further than most. A big early cut often means the seller wants a sale, not a long wait."),
("b12", "owner", "Who owns it? This section covers ownership: the registered owner, how long they have held it, and any recorded loans. The owner is a Florida company, registered since June twenty twenty. The seller is a private person, in a different Florida city. So the report's first advice applies: make sure the registered owner matches the seller. No loan is recorded on the airframe. But an engine can carry its own loan, so check the engine serial number. Then run a title search, and pay through escrow, a neutral party that holds the money."),
("b13", "ads", "What should the logbooks show? This section lists mandatory safety fixes, called airworthiness directives. The report matches eleven to this airplane. Six only apply if certain parts are installed. Two stand out. First, a twenty twenty-four directive to inspect the rear wing spar attachment. The spar is the main beam that holds the wing on. The report flags it past five thousand hours, at about twenty-five hundred dollars. This airplane has over twelve thousand. Second, the Garmin autopilot has its own directive, a software update. A rival Archer's listing says its spar inspection is done. This listing does not mention it. Ask for proof of both."),
("b14", "sdr", "Has it ever been hurt? The listing reports no damage. NextPlane finds no accident record. Reported service problems: about five hundred on this model in five years, mostly cracked brackets and skin. PA-28s tend to fly hard in training, so a large count is expected. None were reported on this airplane."),
("b15", "opcost", "What will it cost to keep? This section estimates a year of ownership. At one hundred twenty hours a year, about twenty-three and a half thousand dollars, or about one hundred ninety-six an hour. Fuel is the biggest line, about seven thousand. Insurance is figured on the full asking price, so get a real quote."),
("b16", "seller", "And who am I dealing with? A private seller, so there is no dealer to stand behind the sale. Budget for your own pre-buy inspection, at a Piper shop you choose. And confirm the seller can sign for the company that owns it."),
("b17", "offer", "Step three. Price the offer. High-hour Archers ask a typical one hundred thousand dollars. Archers with nearly new engines ask about one hundred sixty-seven thousand, with far fewer airframe hours. This one asks one hundred eighty thousand. The seller already cut forty thousand in the first week. NextPlane's model gives close to an even chance of another cut within ninety days. So a reasonable opening offer is around one hundred sixty thousand dollars, about eleven percent under the ask. Make it subject to the pre-buy, the spar inspection record, and the engine rebuild paperwork."),
("b18", "watch", "Step four. Keep watching. In the last two weeks, three new Archers were listed. Sellers made four price cuts, and no increases. Right now, Archer sellers are moving."),
("b19", "verdict", "So who is this airplane right for? A buyer who wants a modern Garmin panel, an autopilot, and a fresh engine, and plans to fly a lot. It is not right for you if you want low airframe hours, or plan to resell soon. Priced well above similar airplanes, with a clean history. Check three things: the spar inspection, the engine paperwork, and what the panel is worth to you."),
("b20", "end", "Before you call the broker, check the data at NextPlane dot U S."),
]
if __name__ == "__main__":
    import re
    n = sum(len(t.split()) for _, _, t in BEATS); print("words", n)
    assert not any("..." in t for _, _, t in BEATS)
    for b, _, t in BEATS:
        for s in re.split(r"(?<=[.?!])\s+", t):
            if len(s.split()) > 22: print("LONG", b, len(s.split()), s)
